Among the following probability models, the number of geometric probability models is (     )  
\\(①\\) Randomly selecting a number from the interval \\([-10,10]\\) and calculating the probability of getting \\(1\\);  
\\(②\\) Randomly selecting a number from the interval \\([-10,10]\\) and calculating the probability of getting a number whose absolute value is no greater than \\(1\\);  
\\(③\\) Randomly selecting an integer from the interval \\([-10,10]\\) and calculating the probability of getting a number greater than \\(1\\) and less than \\(2\\);  

\\(④\\) Throwing a point \\(P\\) into a square \\(ABCD\\) with a side length of \\(4 cm\\) and calculating the probability that point \\(P\\) is no more than \\(1 cm\\) away from the center．
A: \\(1\\)
B: \\(2\\)
C: \\(3\\)
D: \\(4\\)

s:
**Analysis**

Use the characteristics of geometric probability models: the number of basic events is infinite; each outcome is equally likely to occur.

**Solution**

Solution: \\(①\\) is not a geometric probability model, although the interval \\([-10,10]\\) has infinitely many points, getting “\\(1\\)” is just a number and does not constitute a region length;

\\(②\\) is a geometric probability model because there are infinitely many numbers that can be selected from the intervals \\([-10,10]\\) and \\([-1,1]\\) (satisfying infinity), and the chance of each number being selected in these two intervals is equal (satisfying equal likelihood);

\\(③\\) is not a geometric probability model, because there are only \\(21\\) integers in the interval \\([-10,10]\\) (which is finite), not satisfying the characteristic of infinity;

\\(④\\) is a geometric probability model, because there are infinitely many points within a square with a side length of \\(4 cm\\) and a circle with a radius of \\(1 cm\\), and any point within these two areas has an equal chance of being selected, thus satisfying the characteristics of infinity and equal likelihood.

Therefore, the answer is $\boxed{B}$.
